WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.278
Idea. Say your family gives you twenty dollars for lunches each week. By Friday you have spent fifteen, so five is left. To start next week with twenty again, they give you fifteen: exactly what you spent.

00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:28.410
Petty cash works the same way. It is called the imprest system. The petty cashier starts each period with a fixed amount, the float, or imprest. At the end, the float is restored by giving back exactly the amount spent.

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:39.013
Method. Step one: find the amount spent. Either add up the payments, or take the cash left away from the float. That amount is the top-up.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:51.791
Step two: the double entry. Petty cash receives the money, so debit petty cash. The bank pays it out, so credit bank. That means the top-up is on the payments side of the main cash book.

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:57.640
After the top-up, the petty cash is back to the full float, ready for the next period.

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:12.631
Worked example. Here is a real question from November 2025. The float is two hundred and fifty dollars, and one hundred and eighty dollars was spent. Which amount restores the float? One mark.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:20.322
Here are the four options: seventy, one hundred and eighty, two hundred and fifty, and four hundred and thirty dollars.

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:32.749
The cash left is two hundred and fifty minus one hundred and eighty: seventy. To get back to two hundred and fifty, seventy needs one hundred and eighty more. That is the amount spent.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:43.538
So the answer is B. Seventy is the cash left. Two hundred and fifty is the float itself. Four hundred and thirty wrongly adds the spending to the float.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:56.919
The double entry: one hundred and eighty is debited to petty cash, and credited to the bank column of the main cash book. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:06.443
Common mistake. A common mistake is giving the cash left as the top-up. The top-up is the amount spent, not what is left.

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:14.327
Another is reversing the entry. Petty cash receives the money, so petty cash is debited and bank is credited.

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:25.035
Your turn. Now you try. Hana keeps a petty cash float, and some cash is left at the end of June. Answer both parts. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:39.745
Answer. Hana spent three hundred minus sixty-four: two hundred and thirty-six dollars. So the top-up is two hundred and thirty-six. Check: sixty-four plus two hundred and thirty-six is three hundred.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:49.439
For part b, debit petty cash two hundred and thirty-six, and credit bank two hundred and thirty-six, on the payments side of the main cash book.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:58.525
Remember: find the amount spent, top up by exactly that, debit petty cash and credit bank. Now try the practice questions.
